# `undefined > 5` and `NaN > 5` are both false. So an element of a list of numbers whose one user is a relation beside a number is read with NaN for a hole and for what lies past the end: no question
# whether the element is there, no way out of the turn. In a turn that stores, every read asked for the element and left the turn where there was none; what is only held against a number, by a relation
# or an equality, is written nowhere, so it asks no more: the relation takes NaN, the equality takes the number or undefined as the read gives it.
# f1 = over, f2 = tally. The loop of tally removes its check twice: in the function, and in the copy that the loop of the module takes for its one call.
